  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 321 AM CDT Tue Aug 18 2026\n\n(Thursday - Friday)\n\nThe frontal boundary should push further to the south by Thursday, \nwith a weak ridge of surface high pressure moving into the mid-\nMississippi Valley. This should help promote dry conditions with \nnear to below normal temperatures to end the work week.\n\n\n(Friday Night - Monday)\n\nEnsemble guidance remains in pretty good agreement on the general \nsynoptic pattern across the CONUS, with anomalous ridging across \nthe Rockies/south central Plains and a strong trough moving across \nthe Great Lakes early this weekend. There are still some subtle \ndifferences amongst membership of the LREF, but it seems likely that \nour area will be on the western periphery of a cooler air mass \nmoving in behind a cold frontal passage Friday night/Saturday. This \nfront will bring a low chance (20%) for showers and maybe a rumble \nof thunder, but temperatures behind it are more uncertain. The \nspread between the 25th/75th percentile for 850-hPa temperatures on \nthe LREF is quite high Sunday into Monday, generally 5-6C. Not \nsurprisingly, the inter-quartile range of the NBM also shows a large \nspread in high temperatures, with most locations in the 6-10 degree \nrange. For reference, that is roughly the difference between well-\nbelow normal temperatures to slightly above normal values. Monday's \ntemperatures may also be impacted by an increase in mid/high level \nclouds as well as the potential for showers and thunderstorms as a \nmidlevel shortwave trough comes across the northeastern periphery of \nthe ridge. The exact timing and track of this feature of course are \nbig question marks nearly 7 days out.",
